## 1. What the belt actually gives you

Three spectral families. You mine a mix, not a logo. [[171]](grok://citation?card_id=d8552e&card_type=citation_card&type=render_inline_citation&citation_id=171)

| Class | Fraction / role | What you pull |
|---|---|---|
| C-type | Most of the belt | Hydrated silicates, organics, C, some water, modest metal |
| S-type | Common | Silicates + native Fe–Ni, Co, traces of Au / PGM |
| M-type | ~5% of classified | Fe–Ni metal, FeS, silicates; radar-bright, not solid iron |

(16) Psyche is the headline M-type. Density ~3.4–4.2 g/cm³ vs ~7.9 for solid kamacite. Best current read: mixed metal + silicate ± porosity, on the order of **~30–60 vol% metal**, not a polished core you slice into pauldron stock. Surface mixes in the literature run metal-rich with low-Fe pyroxene and exogenous carbonaceous junk. Plan on slag and silicates in every heat. [[172]](grok://citation?card_id=200462&card_type=citation_card&type=render_inline_citation&citation_id=172)

Iron-meteorite analog (the workable assay):

| Element | Typical | Superalloy need |
|---|---|---|
| Fe | ~90 wt% | Matrix or leftover |
| Ni | **5–10 wt%** (range 4–60) | Inconel-class wants **~50–55%** |
| Co | 0.3–1.3 wt% | Useful (Haynes / some Ni alloys) |
| P | 0.1–0.5 wt% | Slag or embrittler — control it |
| S | 0.1–2 wt% as FeS / troilite | Slag. Kills hot work if left in |
| Cr | ppm–0.2 wt% | Inconel 718 wants **~19–21%** |
| Mo | ~3–30 ppm | 718 wants **~3%** |
| Ti | ~ppm | 718 wants **~1%** |
| Al | not a native metal phase | 718 wants **~0.5%** |
| Nb | not available | 718 wants **~5%** |
| PGM (Pt, Ir, Ru…) | ~6–230 ppm in irons | Catalyst / coupon, not plate alloy |

So: belt metal is **kamacite / taenite Fe–5–11Ni**, as-cast yield ~260–440 MPa, ductility falling as Ni rises. That is habitat plate. It is not γ′ nickel superalloy until you add Cr, Al, Ti, Mo, Nb from somewhere else. [[188]](grok://citation?card_id=49d761&card_type=citation_card&type=render_inline_citation&citation_id=188)

